# /hm:make

> Quick re-render or full reconfigure of the harness. Generated by harness-maker.

## Usage

```
/hm:make                — re-render with current settings (recommended after plugin update)
/hm:make --reinterview  — full interactive reconfigure via /harness-maker:make
```

## Procedure

Parse `$ARGUMENTS` for flags.

### Default (no args or `--update`)

Re-render using existing `.claude/harness.yaml` answers, then guide you all the
way to "your harness is in git the way you want." The flow is: resolve the active
plugin → (only if `.claude/` already exists) **preview & confirm** → apply →
narrate what changed → **git disposition**. A fresh `.claude/` skips the preview.

The bash line below discovers which harness-maker plugin install is active and
uses it. Resolution path: a cache-glob bootstraps discovery (works even on first
upgrade from <0.20 where `locate` is not yet available), then `harness-maker
locate --plain` is invoked through that bootstrap to get the **canonical** install
per the priority rules (projectPath==cwd > scope=user > installedAt desc — see
docs/BOOTSTRAP.md + PLAN-locate-cli-version-gate ADR-001). The cache-glob result
is used as fallback when `locate` is absent.

Why both: `/plugin update` bumps the plugin cache but the rendered `make.md` keeps
calling the OLD CLI, which re-emits its OLD pin (the self-update bootstrap trap
fixed in 0.19.1). The cache glob lets us reach the new binary; `locate` then
disambiguates between scopes/projects.

**Step 1 — resolve the active plugin install.** Run this and capture the printed
`HM=` path; use that exact path as `$HM` in every later step (shell state does not
persist between Bash calls):

```bash
!HM=$(ls -1d "$HOME"/.claude/plugins/cache/harness-maker*/harness-maker/[0-9]*.[0-9]*.[0-9]* 2>/dev/null | while read -r p; do printf '%s %s\n' "${p##*/}" "$p"; done | sort -V | tail -1 | cut -d' ' -f2-); HM=$(uv run --with "${HM:-'{{ harness_maker_src_path }}'}" python -m harness_maker.cli locate --plain 2>/dev/null || echo "${HM:-'{{ harness_maker_src_path }}'}"); echo "HM=$HM"
```

**Step 2 — preview (ONLY when `.claude/` already exists and is non-empty).** Show
what the re-render will change before touching the tree — this is why `/hm:make`
does NOT render in a worktree: the CLI backs up to `.backup-<ts>/` and reconcile
preserves your edits, so a read-only preview + confirm is all the safety needed.

```bash
uv run --with "$HM" hm cli make "$(pwd)" --update --dry-run
```

Surface the NEW / REPLACE / KEEP / MERGE counts (KEEP = your edits preserved,
MERGE = block-merged into your edits), then ask with the structured question tool
(`AskUserQuestion` in Claude Code, `AskQuestion` in Cursor) — **Proceed** /
**Cancel**. On Cancel, stop here. If `.claude/` does not exist yet (fresh install),
skip this step and apply directly.

**Step 3 — apply.** Run the real re-render:

```bash
uv run --with "$HM" hm cli make "$(pwd)" --update
```

**Step 4 — narrate what changed.** Parse the `render-summary:` line
(`files=… keep=… merge=… targets=…`) plus the reconcile report from the CLI output,
and tell the user, in their locale: what changed, what was preserved (KEEP/MERGE),
and the current `harness_maker_version`.

**Step 5 — git disposition.** Guide the user's git decision for the generated
harness (**neutral** — present the two options as equals, with no recommended
marker and no preference ordering). Run:

```bash
uv run --with "$HM" hm cli git-status "$(pwd)"
```

Read the JSON and branch on `is_git` / `decision_needed` / `offer_stage` /
`prior_decision`:

- **`is_git: false`** — not a git repository. Tell the user the harness files are
  not under version control; if they want to track them, they can run `git init`
  and re-run `/hm:make`. Run no git commands. Done.
- **`decision_needed: true`** — ask, in the user's locale, the two options **as
  equals** (the order below is not a recommendation):
  - **Commit them** — the harness roots are added to git and committed, so anyone
    who clones the repo gets the harness. On this choice run (scopes both the add
    AND the commit to only the existing roots — the `-- $roots` pathspec keeps any
    unrelated pre-staged work OUT of the harness commit; churn + `.backup-*` are
    already gitignored, so the commit is clean):
    ```bash
    roots=""; for r in .claude .cursor .codex .agents AGENTS.md; do [ -e "$r" ] && roots="$roots $r"; done; git add $roots && git commit -m "chore: add harness-maker harness" -- $roots
    ```
  - **Gitignore them** — the roots are added to `.gitignore` and kept local. On
    this choice run (fails loudly if the write does not take effect):
    ```bash
    uv run --with "$HM" hm cli git-ignore-roots "$(pwd)"
    ```
- **`offer_stage: true`** — already committing, and new harness files appeared on
  this re-render. Ask "Stage the new harness files into a commit?"; on yes,
  `git add` the paths in `untracked_files` then commit **scoped to those paths**:
  `git commit -m "chore: update harness-maker harness" -- <untracked_files>` (the
  `-- <paths>` keeps unrelated staged work out); on no, skip.
- **`prior_decision: "commit"` (no `offer_stage`), `"ignore"`, or no rendered roots
  present** — already decided (or nothing to dispose). Say one line ("harness
  already tracked" / "harness already gitignored — nothing to do") and finish.
  **Do NOT re-prompt** — the decision is inferred from git state, so a re-render
  never re-nags.

### `--reinterview`

Routes to the full interactive setup. Run `/harness-maker:make` instead —
it asks locale first, then drives the structured question tool (`AskQuestion` in Cursor, `AskUserQuestion` in Claude Code) for preset, dev_mode, targets, and
dispatches with the collected flags.

Tell the user:

> To reconfigure your harness interactively, run `/harness-maker:make`.
> That command drives the full interview with smart defaults and a confirm
> screen. `/hm:make` is for quick re-renders only.

Do **not** pass `--reinterview` to the CLI from a slash-command context —
it falls back to autoloop defaults (no TTY). The plugin-level
`/harness-maker:make` handles this correctly via the structured question tool.

